(:id "port/python/vt/wide-scalars" :state :done :evidence ("internal/codegen/pythontable/vtwidescalars_test.go: TestPythonVtWide" "internal/codegen/pythontable/vtwidescalars_test.go: TestPythonVtWideNegativeControl" "make python-vt-wide-negative-control (make/python.mk)" "internal/codegen/pythontable/runtime/_schema/kind_wide.py") :tested-revision "97e13ba1ccc29aa45e4bf9368ce7d67b5ae1860f" :note "2026-11-15: the wide scalars ride their own table runtime module, _schema/kind_wide.py, registered as the wide dispatch entry beside int: fixed(I, F), ufixed(I, F), int128 and uint128 read and write the raw integer, sixteen bytes low half first for the 128-bit kinds, with the descriptor's declared raw range as the write contract and an out-of-range read refused. TestPythonVtWide holds the construct read-then-write against the Go engine byte for byte at every width and both signednesses, at the default raw values, and holds the manifest wide-scalars rows to their verdict; TestPythonVtWideNegativeControl damages the sign decision and the byte order in a copy of the module and goes red under both pins. The manifest wide units (scalars, tblscalars2) also carry a packet type by value and unbounded arrays outside this cut, so the frame refuses those units by name, and widelist's []uint128 is an array element this cut does not carry, so its read refuses by name. Run on Linux x86-64 under the pinned CPython 3.11.18 and 3.15.2 (make dist-python), SCHEMA_SLOW=1, at this revision: make test-python, make python-vt-wide-negative-control, and the done-when TestPythonVtWide command all green.")
